Q: How to calculate magic constants for divide-by-multiply for constant modulo operation A: <p>Thanks to @Jarod42, I was able to find the proper function for calculating the missing value. This uses an example from the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_multiplicative_inverse" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Modular multiplicative inverse</a> wikipedia-article:</p>
<pre><code>struct DivMagic64 {
    int64_t multiply;
    uint64_t compare;
};

constexpr uint64_t modinv64(uint64_t d) 
{
    uint64_t x = d;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)
    {
        x *= 2 - d * x;
    }
    return x;
}

constexpr DivMagic64 computeDivMagic(uint64_t d) {
    assert(d &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; (d &amp; 1)); // only works for odd divisors

    const uint64_t cmp = ~uint64_t(0) / d + 1;

    const int64_t multiply = modinv64(d); // this was the missing factor

    return { multiply, cmp };
}
</code></pre>
<p>This calculates the same two factors that clang uses for their optimization. The only limitation being, that it only works for odd divisors - even ones would require some other calculation for &quot;modinv64&quot; (as well as instructions), which I did not bother to figure out since my case only involves odd divisors. This can the be used to generate the (pseudo)asm:</p>
<pre><code>const auto magic = computeDivMagic(number);

// for full syntax, refer to OP
imul  rdi, magic.multiply
cmp   rdi, magic.compare
setb  al

// equivalent to:
xor   edx, edx
div   number    // assuming input in RAX
test  rdx, rdx
setz  al        // return   remainder == 0
</code></pre>

Q: Unable to get the actual remaining time from rate limiter A: <h3>About the error</h3>
<p>OP asked for a wrong time returned by <code>RateLimiter::availableIn()</code> but, the key provided to the method is wrong, thus it returns <code>0</code>. I suspect that the seconds OP sees in the resulting error is some default value which is substituted when <code>seconds</code> is <code>0</code>.</p>
<h3>How to compute the key</h3>
<p><strong>DISCLAIMER</strong>: the answer has been tested with Laravel &gt;= 10.</p>
<p>To get seconds until next retry with <code>RateLimiter::availableIn()</code>, the key should be computed by combining <code>RateLimiter::for</code> argument with <code>Limit::by</code> argument:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>use Illuminate\Cache\RateLimiting\Limit;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\RateLimiter;

$rateLimiterName = 'myRateLimiter';

RateLimiter::for($rateLimiterName, function (Request $request) use ($rateLimiterName) {
    $throttleKey = 'some value based on request';

    return Limit::perMinutes(5)
        -&gt;by($throttleKey)
        -&gt;response(function() use ($rateLimiterName, $throttleKey) {
            // See below for Laravel &gt;= 10.13.3
            $key = md5($rateLimiterName.$throttleKey);
            $availableIn = RateLimiter::availableIn($key);
        });
});
</code></pre>
<p>For Laravel &gt;= 10.13.3 there's <code>\Illuminate\Routing\Middleware\ThrottleRequests::$shouldHashKeys</code> which modifies the way the key to pass to <code>RateLimiter::availableIn()</code> is computed: if it's <code>false</code>, the key is simply <code>$rateLimiterName.':'.$throttleKey</code>.</p>
<p>Note that this still holds for Laravel 12.</p>
<p>Source: <a href="https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/56ad777128b35ff55a1ea4df1c269ba2daabe57c/src/Illuminate/Routing/Middleware/ThrottleRequests.php#L130" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Laravel Framework Code</a></p>
